.Dd June 10, 2026
.Dt LIBCAPABILITY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_daemon_run ,
.Nm cap_daemon_recv ,
.Nm cap_daemon_send ,
.Nm cap_daemon_label_allowed
.Nd helpers for writing .cap bundle service daemons
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libcapability
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In capability.h
.Bd -literal
#define CAP_LABEL_MAX 64

typedef int (*cap_client_handler)(int, const char *, void *);

struct cap_daemon_config {
    const char         *service_name;
    cap_client_handler  handler;
    void               *handler_arg;
    unsigned            client_timeout;
};
.Ed
.Ft int
.Fn cap_daemon_run "const struct cap_daemon_config *cfg"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn cap_daemon_recv "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t bufsz" "unsigned timeout"
.Ft int
.Fn cap_daemon_send "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t len"
.Ft bool
.Fn cap_daemon_label_allowed "const char *path" "const char *label"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libcapability
library provides a turnkey daemon lifecycle for services shipped as
.Pa .cap
bundles and managed by
.Xr serviced 8 .
It wraps
.Xr libservice 3
initialization, capprotect shielding, service registration, signal
handling, and a forking accept loop into a single call, allowing
bundle authors to focus on per-client protocol logic.
.Ss Daemon lifecycle
.Fn cap_daemon_run
is the main entry point.
The caller populates a
.Vt "struct cap_daemon_config"
and passes it in:
.Bl -tag -width "client_timeout" -offset indent
.It Va service_name
A reverse-domain service name
.Pq e.g., Dq org.5bsd.kldmgrd
to register with
.Xr serviced 8 .
Must not be
.Dv NULL .
.It Va handler
A
.Vt cap_client_handler
callback invoked in a forked child for each connecting client.
The callback receives the client file descriptor, the client's label
string, and the opaque
.Va handler_arg .
It must return 0 on success or non-zero on failure.
Must not be
.Dv NULL .
.It Va handler_arg
An opaque pointer forwarded to
.Va handler .
May be
.Dv NULL .
.It Va client_timeout
Seconds before an
.Xr alarm 3
fires in the child process.
A value of 0 selects the default timeout of 30 seconds.
.El
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_run
performs the following steps in order:
.Bl -enum -offset indent
.It
Calls
.Fn service_init
to obtain the pair file descriptor inherited from
.Xr serviced 8 .
.It
Applies capprotect shielding via
.Fn service_protect .
A failure with
.Er ENOTSUP
is silently ignored; other errors are logged but do not abort.
.It
Registers
.Va service_name
with the
.Xr serviced 8
naming registry.
.It
Sends a readiness notification via
.Fn service_ready .
.It
Installs signal handlers:
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGINT
trigger a clean shutdown;
.Dv SIGCHLD
triggers reaping of child processes.
.It
Enters a blocking accept loop.
For each client connection,
.Fn fork
is called and the child invokes
.Va handler .
.El
.Pp
The function returns when a quit signal is received or an
initialization step fails.
.Ss Message I/O
.Fn cap_daemon_recv
receives a message from the mac_capability pair file descriptor
.Fa fd
into
.Fa buf ,
reading up to
.Fa bufsz
bytes.
If
.Fa timeout
is non-zero, an
.Xr alarm 3
is set before and reset after the underlying
.Fn service_recv
call, so the child process will be terminated if the client
stalls.
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_send
sends
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa buf
on the file descriptor
.Fa fd .
It wraps
.Fn service_send
and logs a warning via
.Xr syslog 3
on failure.
.Ss Allow-list checking
.Fn cap_daemon_label_allowed
checks whether the client label
.Fa label
appears in the allow-list file at
.Fa path .
The file is read line by line; comments beginning with
.Ql #
and surrounding whitespace are stripped.
The function returns
.Dv true
if any non-empty line matches
.Fa label
exactly, or if a line consists of the single wildcard character
.Ql * .
If the file does not exist or
.Fa label
is not found, the function returns
.Dv false .
A missing file
.Pq Er ENOENT
is treated silently; other open errors are logged via
.Xr syslog 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn cap_daemon_run
returns 0 when a quit signal is received after a successful
accept loop, or \-1 if an initialization step fails, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_recv
returns the number of bytes received on success, or \-1 on
failure, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_send
returns 0 on success, or \-1 on failure, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_label_allowed
returns
.Dv true
if the label is allowed, or
.Dv false
otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Minimal .cap bundle daemon:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <capability.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

static int
handle_client(int fd, const char *label, void *arg)
{
    char buf[256];
    ssize_t n;

    n = cap_daemon_recv(fd, buf, sizeof(buf), 0);
    if (n <= 0)
        return (1);

    /* Echo the message back. */
    return (cap_daemon_send(fd, buf, (size_t)n));
}

int
main(void)
{
    struct cap_daemon_config cfg = {
        .service_name = "org.example.echo",
        .handler = handle_client,
    };

    return (cap_daemon_run(&cfg) == 0 ? 0 : 1);
}
.Ed
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn cap_daemon_run
calls
.Xr fork 2
for each client connection.
The child inherits a copy of the parent's address space, so the
handler must not modify shared state.
.Pp
.Fn cap_daemon_label_allowed
opens and reads the allow-list file on every call.
Services that require high-frequency checks should consider
caching the result.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr alarm 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr kldmgrd 8 ,
.Xr rebootd 8 ,
.Xr serviced 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm libcapability
first appeared in 5BSD.
